00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
hi there and thanks for joining this  presentation i'm chris deeks one of the  field sales managers at cooled  and today i'll be talking and discussing  led illumination in life science  research  specifically for fluorescence microscopy  in this presentation i'll look at three  main points we'll see why leds are the  now the go-to option for fluorescence  microscopy  and the benefits they provide look at  why they originally had some  shortcomings compared to mercury lamps  and how these have been overcome  and actually two questions regarding  these shortcomings often come up  firstly a wavelength available for my  chosen flora4 and secondly is it bright  enough  so i'll explain why these are actually  hangovers of when the technology was  still maturing  and finally i'll look at how leds are  not just a light source replacement for  a mercury lamp that can just be put on a  system  but how they can actually be fully  involved in a smart system setup and  help provide better imaging for the user  so at cooled we work solely with leds as  a light emitter  and these leds come with large benefits  one of them being a long lifetime  compared to mercury bulbs  with leds having a lifetime of over  twenty thousand hours when used in this  manner  leds themselves actually have a life  expectancy of over a hundred thousand  hours  but to get the extra brightness out of  them we over drive them slightly  this is absolutely fine to do as long as  they're cooled sufficiently  which we do in our products mercury  bulbs however have a lifetime of about  200 hours before they become a lot  dimmer compared to when they were new  they can last longer than this but the  more it's used the dim of the output  which then restricts the ability to get  high quality images from experiments  over a longer time period leds also  don't have any warm up or cool down time  which mercury bulbs do  leds can be switched on and off as much  as is needed without any problems  whereas with mercury bulbs they require  about 20 minutes to warm up before they  reach their most bright  and then when switching them off they  have to stay off for around half hour or  so before they can be switched on again  this means actually most of the time the  lamps are kept on during the day in labs  just in case anyone needs to use them  this actually deteriorates them quickly  and also cuts short their lifetime  mercury lamps also give a broad white  spectrum whereas leds give much narrower  bands  and these often come with independent  channel intensity control as well  so if a wavelength is not needed those  leds can remain switched off  or if several leds are in an  illumination system they can easily be  adjusted to change the intensity  which makes it perfect for balancing  fluorophores on samples without the need  for an nd filter  here you can see a typical setup of an  old style mercury lamp with a lot of  cumbersome features that are not  required  when working with led sources an  electric shutter is no longer needed as  leds can be switched on and off with no  warm-up time is just discussed  nd filters aren't needed due to the  precise intensity control in led units  and as leds provide narrow bandwidths of  light excitation filters can now be  placed into units in front of their  respective leds  to prevent excitation filter wheels  being needed  these benefits give a cost reduction but  also prevent things like vibration which  can occur  from all these mechanical parts in a  setup it can also provide much faster  imaging than was previously possible as  well  leds are now the preferred option for  lighting on microscopes  and they now fit most old and new models  microscopes do have different optical  path lens but this can be dealt with in  several ways  for example in our p300 series direct  fit model we have an adjuster that moves  an optical lens  so the light reaching the sample can  provide the best homogeneity  with our liquid light guide units these  typically use a collimator which can be  adjusted  again to improve the brightness and  homogeneity on the sample between all  the different microscopes  as it isn't one size fits all so i  mentioned at the beginning of this  presentation  two questions often come up when people  were still unsure about using leds  so look at these now  first one of these questions was  regarding leds and coverage of the  wavelengths  so here you can see a chart showing the  available wavelengths on our pe 100 unit  where any single led can be chosen from  a range of 20.  we can see the range here from 365  nanometers in the uv  up to 770 nanometers in the infrared  however our p340 fury unit also provides  a 340 nanometer led  for applications requiring this  wavelength so we can go further into the  uv if required  this range of wavelengths provides the  ability to work with the majority of  stains  in the research and clinical fields  however leds themselves can actually go  far beyond this  in the infrared to about 1700 nanometers  and down  into the uv to wavelengths of around 260  nanometers  for applications such as sterilization  these aren't typically used in more  routine microscopy  applications at the moment but this  could change  one weakness in leds is they do have  less power in the green region  but as you can see in the graph here one  way to aid this is to create a broader  peak  so this is done by fostering an led  which gives us this broadness  so at cool ed we use a very strong blue  led with the phosphor on top  which is very efficient and gives very  good brightness other methods include  using a laser to excite the phosphor  this would obviously require a laser in  the system which can add cost  and could be a health and safety issue  as well but as you can see it's clear  leds do cover a broad spectral region  but often on microscopes more than one  wavelength is needed to look at  different fluorophores  so it's common for led units to be  fitted with more than one led  it's quite common for them at the moment  to have three or four in in fact one of  our units the p4000 is fitted with 16  leds  giving a broad array of wavelengths  available to use  but even with a broad choice of  wavelengths it's important to match the  leds in the units to the excitation  filters in a microscope  to get the best image as possible  the second question often asked is are  leds bright enough  and here we can see a comparison of a  mercury lamp the gray line  compared to one of our p300 series shown  by the blue line  which has three leds fitted the power of  leds has actually greatly increased over  the past years so back in 2006  uh when cool had first released that led  source for fluorescence microscopy  we were at a tiny fraction of the power  at now we can see from the graph here  how we can  compare now with leds being much  brighter in the uv region  and in the blue but the red is slightly  lower than the lamp power still  it's much more flat though and provides  more than enough power for fluorophores  in this region  we actually upgraded this unit a couple  of years ago with new leds providing  over  double the power output compared to the  original leds we used  in fact led power is roughly doubling  every two to three years or so  so it's going to be a matter of time  before they're even brighter in the red  and all the other regions as well  we've actually been told by some people  though that led power is more than  enough  now and i've seen talks and papers  suggesting can be better to excite  flora4s with less power  but for an extended time period so some  people do still want the power though  but we can say  obviously the power is there one thing  to remember is when the power is quoted  this can be  quite a confusing element often it's not  the power that's measured at the sample  itself  so we actually tend to use the word  irradiance and this is actually the  power  over the area and this is quoted in  milliwatts per millimeter squared  this gives a much a better idea of  what's happening at the sample which is  where the graph here was actually  measured as well occasionally you may  see the power quoted as out of a light  guide as well  but this doesn't reflect the light that  actually makes it to the sample so it  can be misleading as it doesn't factor  in variable losses through the light  path  of different microscopes and we've  actually created a couple of white  papers recently explaining how to  accurately and precisely measure  irradiance  so if you're interested you can find  this either on our website or available  to download on our booth as well  so we've discussed the convenience of  leds providing the long lifetime the  instant on off and the fact there's no  mercury  but leds actually offer many additional  benefits in terms of the performance and  quality of data that can be collected  so leds are not just a dumb lamp like  you get with mercury  but can actually provide sophisticated  control and can lead to  improvements in the image by improving  the signal to noise  it can also help reduce photo bleaching  meaning it can  image for longer and improve the cell  viability as well  this actually comes down to the narrow  bandwidth of the leds and the high speed  of the control you can get  you can take a couple of looks at some  examples now  so here's an image showing the reason  why the signal to noise is improved  as you can see the gray line is a  spectrum from the metal halide lamp  and the blue is the 435 nanometer led  bulb  for exciting something like cfp you can  actually see how well  this fits into the excitation filter  pass band shown by the solid  red line on the left there's actually  very little of the led energy that falls  into the emission  filter pass band that's important for  reducing the background and improving  the signal-to-noise ratio  so the optical filters provide a great  job obviously blocking the light from  unwanted wavelengths  but with the narrow bands from the led  it improves the data even further by  doing this compared to  mercury lamps narrow band of the led  means  less uv light is also reaching your  sample as well  so for every uv photon that reaches your  sample it can be causing background  fluorescence  and can be causing damage to samples as  well  so using leds can have quite big  advantages for data collection  and actually a study by sandrine prost  in 2016  showed some nice signal-to-noise  improvements of between two and seven  times with leds compared to lamp-based  sources  which is pretty incredible to see  so in terms of improving cell viability  this comes down to high speed switching  in fact one of the biggest improvements  is about being able to synchronize the  on and off with the exposure of the  camera  with the led light source and this can  be done via usb  if the light source is integrated into  the software or via a ttl line from the  camera to an led light source  so reducing or removing the needless  light illumination from either side of  the camera exposure  goes a big way to reduce photo bleaching  from when shutters were used  or in fact when fast switching isn't  used at all and this will improve cell  viability  one method that has been discussed as  well is to use very fast pulsing  rather than continuous illumination this  can be a  few microseconds pulse followed by a  rest time to allow the excited  fluorophores to come back down to ground  state  and again followed by another pulse and  so on this has been shown to improve  cell viability and reduce photo  bleaching  you can actually see how this was  studied in the paper referenced here  so leds can be used not only for  fluorescence applications but for  transmitted ones as well  typically halogen bulbs have been used  for transmitted light  this causes a color change when the  intensity is altered  with leds the color temperature stays  the same with varying intensity  giving much more repeatable results for  experiments  with halogen users also like to keep the  lamp on about a 9 volt setting which  gives a color temperature of around 3000  so keeping the cold temperature at this  with leds means the intensity can be  varied  without affecting any work that's being  done  another element that's important for  transmitted light leds  is the correlated color temperature and  that's achieved by balancing the led  and giving it a good green and red  region a poor led is very cold to the  eye with higher intensity in the blue  but leds are also much more favorable in  the uv as well  and it gives minimal illumination in  this wavelength region  so it's much easier on the eye than a  halogen bulb which has a higher  irradiance in the uv  which can cause things like dryness of  the eye  so in summary we've seen leds have huge  benefits over traditional lamps  now more than ever with their increasing  brightness and their large range of leds  that are now available  so they give cost savings as well as  providing ease of use  and can provide better quality imaging  as well  so the future of leds also looks good  with ever improving brightness meaning  irradiance will improve  and make data collecting even easier for  the user  so thanks everyone for listening and  thanks to andor for organizing this  conference  feel free to drop by our booth either  myself or or my colleagues will be  available to answer any questions  or you can drop me an email or give us a  call using the information here as well  so thank you very much

